side spending against him, and markets pricing his reelection near 96 percent. With a first-time, low-funded opponent and no adverse developments, fundamentals support a double-digit Democratic margin.", "watch": "A sharp late national swing toward Republicans or a wave of outside spending against Sorensen could tighten the race, though nothing in the current picture suggests either is materializing.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Eric Sorensen (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Illinois' 17th District, a D+3 seat spanning northwest and central Illinois, pairs third-term incumbent Eric Sorensen against Republican Dillan Vancil. Sorensen, a former TV meteorologist first elected in 2022, has built his profile on constituent service, weather and farm issues, and bipartisan work such as a weather enterprise caucus launched with a Nebraska Republican. Vancil won his primary in March but has drawn little attention beyond a skeptical local editorial.\n\nThe incumbent's advantages are wide. He has raised $2.2 million to Vancil's $309,000 and holds a cash edge above $1.2 million, with no significant outside spending on either side. All ten expert ratings consulted call the seat Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets give Sorensen roughly a 96 percent chance of another term. With no public general-election polling available, the race rests on fundamentals, and those point firmly toward Sorensen.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9843, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9781, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9731, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9707, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.973, "margin": 14.9}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9785, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9843, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2